short.modelAnswer:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdint>
int main() {
    std::ios::sync_with_stdio(false);  // stop syncing iostreams with C stdio
    std::cin.tie(nullptr);             // don't flush cout before every cin read
    int n; std::cin >> n;
    long long sum = 0;                 // int64_t: the sum of many ints can overflow 32 bits
    for (int i = 0, x; i < n; ++i) { std::cin >> x; sum += x; }
    std::cout << sum << '\n';          // '\n', not std::endl (which forces a flush)
}
```

`sync_with_stdio(false)` lets `cin` and `cout` use their own buffers instead of going through C stdio on each operation. After it you must not mix `printf` and `cout`. `cin.tie(nullptr)` removes the automatic flush of `cout` before every input operation. Using `'\n'` instead of `std::endl` avoids a flush (a write syscall) per line.

short.q:
Find the bugs in this snippet and fix them:

    int main() {
        int n, sum;
        scanf("%d", n);
        int a[n];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) { scanf("%d", &a[i]); sum += a[i]; }
        printf("%d", sum);
    }

short.modelAnswer:
1. `scanf("%d", n)` passes the value of `n` instead of its address. scanf writes to a garbage address, which is UB. Fix: `scanf("%d", &n)`.
2. `sum` is uninitialised, and reading it is UB. Fix: `int sum = 0;`, or better `long long sum = 0;` to avoid overflow.
3. `i <= n` is an off-by-one that reads and writes `a[n]`, one past the end (UB). Fix: `i < n`.
4. `int a[n]` with a run-time `n` is a VLA, a GCC extension and not standard C++, and a large `n` overflows the stack. Fix: `std::vector<int> a(n);`.

```cpp
int n; if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1) return 1;
std::vector<int> a(n); long long sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { scanf("%d", &a[i]); sum += a[i]; }
printf("%lld\n", sum);
```
